  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Sharing</h1>
  <p class="lede">One budget can be shared with another iCloud user. A second budget can sit beside the first. Those are different things.</p>

  <h2>Share a budget</h2>
  <p>Share from the budget’s own sharing control. The other person accepts the invite on their device, signed into their own iCloud account. They see that budget. The exchange goes through iCloud. ZxBudget is not in the middle, and we cannot read what you shared.</p>
  <p>On the first screen of a new install, <strong>I'm joining a shared budget</strong> waits for that invite. Nothing is created on the device until the shared budget arrives.</p>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>A second budget</h2>
      <p>You can keep more than one budget, for example a household budget and a separate one. Switch from the <strong>···</strong> menu, or on Mac with Switch Budget. They do not mix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img src="/images/guides/switch-budget.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The more menu, with Switch Budget and Settings.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Family Sharing is the subscription</h2>
  <p>Apple Family Sharing lets up to 6 people use the subscription when one person pays. Each person still has their own iCloud account. That does not share a budget with them. Sharing a budget is the step above.</p>
</article>
